# Units and Measurement: The Language of Physics

> Every physical statement pairs a number with a unit. This lesson
> assembles the toolkit — SI base units, prefixes, significant
> figures, dimensional analysis, and errors.

## 1. Why Units Matter

A number without a unit is half a measurement — a wire "10 long"
could be 10 millimetres or 10 metres. In 1999 NASA lost the Mars
Climate Orbiter to a metric–imperial mix-up between its teams.

[definition]
A **physical quantity** is any measurable property written as a
number together with a unit — length, time, temperature, current.
[/definition]

## 2. The Seven SI Base Units

All of physics is built from seven **base quantities** of the
**Système International (SI)**, adopted in 1960.

| Base quantity | Unit | Symbol | Anchoring constant |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Length | metre | m | speed of light, c |
| Mass | kilogram | kg | Planck constant, h |
| Time | second | s | caesium-133 frequency |
| Electric current | ampere | A | elementary charge, e |
| Temperature | kelvin | K | Boltzmann constant, k |
| Amount of substance | mole | mol | Avogadro constant |
| Luminous intensity | candela | cd | luminous efficacy |

[note title="The 2019 redefinition"]
Since 20 May 2019 every base unit is defined by fixing a
fundamental constant — the kilogram no longer depends on a
metal cylinder in Paris.
[/note]

@diagram tree id="si-unit-family" layout="tree" aria="Physical quantities split into base and derived units"
  "Physical quantity" -> "Base: metre, kilogram, second"
  "Physical quantity" -> "Base: ampere, kelvin, mole, candela"
  "Physical quantity" -> "Derived: newton, joule, watt, pascal"
@/diagram

## 3. Derived Units and Prefixes

**Derived units** combine base units; **prefixes** scale them from
nuclear to cosmic sizes.

| Quantity | Definition | SI expression | Name |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Speed | distance ÷ time | m/s | — |
| Force | mass × acceleration | kg·m/s² | newton (N) |
| Pressure | force ÷ area | N/m² | pascal (Pa) |
| Work, energy | force × distance | N·m | joule (J) |
| Power | work ÷ time | J/s | watt (W) |

| Prefix | Symbol | Factor | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| nano | n | 10⁻⁹ | green light ≈ 550 nm |
| micro | μ | 10⁻⁶ | red blood cell ≈ 7 μm |
| milli | m | 10⁻³ | medicine doses in mL |
| kilo | k | 10³ | city distances in km |
| mega | M | 10⁶ | plant output in MW |
| giga | G | 10⁹ | processor clocks in GHz |

[example]
A human hair is about 8 × 10⁻⁵ m wide, and 10⁻⁶ m = 1 μm, so
the hair measures 80 μm — an easy laboratory quantity.
[/example]

## 4. Significant Figures

**Significant figures** record how precisely a value is known.

| Situation | Rule | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Non-zero digits | all significant | 3.42 → 3 s.f. |
| Zeros between non-zeros | significant | 1002 → 4 s.f. |
| Leading zeros | never significant | 0.021 → 2 s.f. |
| Trailing zero after decimal | significant | 2.50 → 3 s.f. |

[important title="Rounding rules"]
Add or subtract → fewest decimal places: 1.2 + 1.25 = 2.5.
Multiply or divide → fewest significant figures: 2.1 × 2.05 = 4.3.
[/important]

## 5. Dimensional Analysis

The **dimensions** of a quantity describe its make-up in mass (M),
length (L), and time (T).

| Quantity | Dimensions | SI unit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Force | MLT⁻² | N |
| Work, energy | ML²T⁻² | J |
| Power | ML²T⁻³ | W |
| Pressure | ML⁻¹T⁻² | Pa |

Dimensions test whether an equation is **dimensionally
homogeneous** — in v² = u² + 2as both sides reduce to L²T⁻².

[warning]
Dimensional analysis cannot find dimensionless constants such as
the 1/2 in kinetic energy or π in the pendulum formula — it
checks a skeleton, not the coefficients.
[/warning]

## 6. Errors, Instruments, and Standards

**Error** is the deviation of a measured value from the true value.

@math label="eq:errors"
\text{percentage error} = \frac{\text{absolute error}}{\text{true value}} \times 100
@/math

[example]
True length 2.00 m, measured 2.04 m: absolute error 0.04 m,
relative error 0.04 ÷ 2.00 = 0.02, percentage error 2%.
[/example]

| Concept | Meaning | Archery picture |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Accuracy | closeness to the true value | hits near the bullseye |
| Precision | closeness of repeated readings | hits clustered tightly |

A precise instrument can still be inaccurate if it carries a fixed
**systematic error** such as a wrongly zeroed scale. A **vernier
caliper** has a **least count** of 0.1 mm; a **screw gauge** with
1 mm pitch and 100 divisions reads to 0.01 mm. India's National
Physical Laboratory (NPL) in New Delhi keeps the standards.

## 7. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| SI base units | seven: m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd |
| nano / kilo / giga | 10⁻⁹ / 10³ / 10⁹ |
| Dimensions of force | MLT⁻² |
| Least counts | vernier 0.1 mm; screw gauge 0.01 mm |
